Let $\Sigma$ be a finite alphabet and let $k, n \geq 2$ be integers. Two finite words $u, v \in \Sigma^*$ are said to be strongly $k$-abelian equivalent, written $u \sim_k v$, if either $|u|, |v| < k$ and $u = v$, or $|u|, |v| \geq k$ and the following hold:
(i) $u$ and $v$ have the same prefix of length $k-1$;
(ii) $u$ and $v$ have the same suffix of length $k-1$;
(iii) for every word $f \in \Sigma^k$, the number of occurrences of $f$ as a factor of $u$ equals the number of occurrences of $f$ as a factor of $v$.

A word $w \in \Sigma^*$ is called a strongly $k$-abelian $n$-th power if $w$ can be written as a concatenation $w = w_1 w_2 \cdots w_n$ of nonempty words such that $w_i \sim_k w_j$ for all $1 \leq i, j \leq n$. A word $u \in \Sigma^*$ is said to avoid strongly $k$-abelian $n$-th powers if no factor of $u$ is a strongly $k$-abelian $n$-th power.

For each finite alphabet $\Sigma$ and each pair of integers $k, n \geq 2$, determine the supremum (and, when finite, the exact value) of the lengths of finite words over $\Sigma$ that avoid strongly $k$-abelian $n$-th powers.

